<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> ski-in/ski-out lodging lets you ski to your door and click into your skis right outside, with no walk, shuttle, or drive to the lifts. It's the most convenient option and usually the most expensive. Listings use the term loosely, so always ask exactly how you get to the nearest lift and back at the end of the day.</p></div>

<h2>Ski-in/ski-out vs slopeside vs walk-to-lift vs shuttle</h2>
<table>
  <caption>Ski-in/ski-out vs slopeside vs walk-to-lift vs shuttle lodging</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Term</th><th scope="col">What it usually means</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Ski-in/ski-out</th><td>You can ski from the door to a lift and ski back to the door without removing your skis.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Slopeside</th><td>Right next to the slopes or base area, but you may need to walk a short distance or cross a road.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Walk-to-lift</th><td>A walk to the nearest lift, which can be a few minutes or much longer in ski boots.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Shuttle access</th><td>You ride a bus or shuttle to the base area.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Why listings can be misleading</h2>
<p>There's no single official definition, so the label gets stretched. "Ski-in/ski-out" might mean you can ski <em>in</em> at the end of the day but have to walk or take a surface lift <em>out</em> in the morning. It might depend on a single run that only opens with enough snow, or require crossing a road or a long flat traverse.</p>
<p>Questions to ask before you book:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Which lift or run is closest, and exactly how do I get there in the morning?</li>
  <li>How do I get back at the end of the day? Is the return run groomed, and what difficulty is it?</li>
  <li>Does access depend on a run that needs good snow coverage to open?</li>
  <li>Is there ski storage or a boot room on-site?</li>
  <li>Are there stairs, roads, or flats to cross with gear?</li>
</ul>
<p>Look at the resort trail map alongside the listing's location to confirm.</p>

<h2>Other lodging terms to know</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Base village:</strong> lodging, shops, and restaurants clustered at the bottom of the lifts. Often walkable to lifts.</li>
  <li><strong>Mid-mountain:</strong> lodging partway up the mountain, sometimes reachable only by lift or snowcat in winter.</li>
  <li><strong>Ski valet or boot room:</strong> a service or room where you store gear near the slopes, which can make walk-to-lift lodging feel close to ski-in/ski-out.</li>
  <li><strong>"Steps to the lift":</strong> marketing language that can mean anything from one minute to ten. Ask for the actual distance and route.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pros and cons</h2>
<h3>Pros</h3>
<ul>
  <li>No hauling gear, kids, or cold feet through parking lots.</li>
  <li>Easy to take breaks, have lunch at your place, or get first chair.</li>
  <li>More time on snow, which matters on short trips.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Cons</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Usually the most expensive lodging at a resort.</li>
  <li>Can be quieter or farther from town restaurants and nightlife.</li>
  <li>Access may change with conditions early or late in the season.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When it's worth it</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Families with young kids,</strong> who make every walk to the lift harder. See the <%= link_to "family ski trip guide", guide_path("family-ski-trip") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Short trips,</strong> where every hour on snow counts.</li>
  <li><strong>Powder chasers</strong> who want to be first in line after a storm; see <%= link_to "how to chase powder", guide_path("how-to-chase-powder") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Beginners</strong> who tire quickly and want an easy way to take breaks.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you're on a budget or want nightlife, walk-to-lift or in-town lodging with a good shuttle can be a smart trade-off.</p>

<h2>Getting close for less</h2>
<p>If true ski-in/ski-out is out of budget, you can get much of the convenience for less:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Walk-to-lift units with a boot room or ski valet</strong> let you store gear at the base and walk over in regular shoes.</li>
  <li><strong>Lodging on a free shuttle route</strong> with frequent service can be nearly as easy, especially in towns built around a resort.</li>
  <li><strong>Off-peak weeks</strong> often bring slopeside prices down closer to in-town rates.</li>
  <li><strong>Splitting a larger unit</strong> with another family or group can make a ski-in/ski-out condo affordable per person.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to find real ski-in/ski-out lodging</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Start with the resort's own lodging pages, which often describe access accurately.</li>
  <li>Filter listings for ski-in/ski-out, then check each location on the trail map.</li>
  <li>Read reviews for comments about the walk to the lift or the return run.</li>
  <li>Ask the host or property the questions above before booking.</li>
  <li>Book early for holiday weeks, when true ski-in/ski-out units sell out first.</li>
</ol>
